  "origin": "Huang & Rothwell (UCL) 2005; clinical iTBS (THREE-D, Toronto/Vancouver; SNT, Stanford)",
  "uT": 5,
  "summary": "Copies intermittent theta-burst timing (Huang et al. 2005): pulse triplets at 50 Hz repeated at 5 Hz in 2-s trains every 10 s, 20 trains and 600 pulses, on one coil at F3 over the left DLPFC, the clinical iTBS target. At uT strength this is a rhythm pattern, not theta-burst stimulation.",
  "notes": "Suppa et al. 2008 define iTBS as 3 pulses at 50 Hz repeated at 5 Hz, 20 trains of 2 s every 10 s (600 pulses, usually quoted as 190 s; strictly 192 s), at 80% of active motor threshold. At TMS strength iTBS is an established depression treatment (THREE-D, Blumberger et al. 2018, 414 patients: non-inferior to 10 Hz rTMS). TMS induces about 100 V/m in cortex, roughly 10^5 times this helmet's induced field, and rodent low-intensity iTBS effects were at mT strength (Tang 2016; Dufor 2019). Nothing is known at uT: the evidence rating applies to iTBS at TMS strength, not to this pattern, which copies the timing only and is not a treatment. The timing is exact; each TMS pulse (about 300-us biphasic) becomes +A for 0.5 ms then -A for 0.5 ms, since shorter shapes cannot be resolved at 2 kHz. F3 is the skull-landmark site for the left DLPFC; Fox et al. 2012 found skull-landmark sites (F3, the 5-cm rule) the least anticorrelated with the subgenual cingulate, and list the Fitzgerald 2009 TMS target at MNI (-46,45,38) and the average 5-cm site at (-41,16,54). The variant lowfield-itbs-pattern-cluster adds AF3 and F5 at half strength. There is no fade-in or fade-out, so all 20 trains run at full strength. Do not copy accelerated schedules (SNT: 1800 pulses per session, 10 sessions a day at 50-min intervals); there is no evidence for them at uT. Not for people with epilepsy; stop if you feel unwell.",
  "setting": "Seated, awake.",
  "schedule": "One 192-s block (600 pulses) per sitting. Do not copy accelerated clinical schedules; nothing is known at uT.",
  "targets": ["left-dlpfc"],
  "source": "Huang et al. 2005, Neuron https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15664172/ ; Suppa et al. 2008, J Physiol (iTBS and cTBS definitions) https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2614023/ ; Blumberger et al. 2018, Lancet (THREE-D) https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29726344/ ; Fox et al. 2012 (left DLPFC targets) https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4120275/ ; Cole et al. 2022, Am J Psychiatry (SNT) https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34711062/ ; Tang et al. 2016, Neuroscience (low-intensity iTBS in vitro) https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27568058/ ; Dufor et al. 2019, Sci Adv (rodent low-intensity rTMS) https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6821463/",
